<BaseLayout
  title="Free Cleaning Invoice Template (Printable, Fill-In) — Main Street Toolbox"
  description="What a cleaning invoice needs in 2026: line-item format, payment terms that actually get you paid, recurring vs one-time examples — plus a free generator that prints a clean one-page invoice, no signup."
  path="/guides/cleaning-invoice-template/"
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Template & guide · {updated}</p>
    <h1>Cleaning invoice template: what to include (and what gets you paid faster)</h1>
    <p><b>The short answer:</b> a one-page invoice with your business name, the client, the date, each service
    as its own priced line, and payment terms at the bottom. No software needed. Below is the exact line-item
    format, the payment terms that actually work, and a <a href="/cleaning/cleaning-invoice-generator/">free
    invoice generator</a> if you'd rather fill in blanks than build a document.</p>

    <h2>The template</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>What goes in it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Your header</td><td>Business name, phone, email. Logo optional — a clean text header is fine.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Client block</td><td>Client name and the service address (they differ for landlords and property managers).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Date + invoice #</td><td>Invoice # can just be the date plus a sequence (0912-01). Needed for your own bookkeeping.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Service lines</td><td>Each task as its own line with its own price — never one line that says "cleaning".</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Total + terms</td><td>Total due, and when: "Due on receipt" or "Net 15".</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Why line items matter more than the total</h2>
    <p>An invoice that reads <b>"Cleaning — $200"</b> invites scope arguments and price shopping. The same clean
    itemized — <i>Recurring clean $95 · Inside oven $35 · Inside fridge $30 · Pet hair add-on $25 · Interior
    windows $15</i> — reads as work, not as a number to negotiate. Clients who can see the parts rarely dispute
    the whole.</p>
    <p>Line items also become your service record. For recurring clients, next month's invoice is the same
    document with a new date — and if a client questions a charge in March, you have February on paper.</p>

    <h2>Payment terms that actually get you paid</h2>
    <p><b>One-time and first-time cleans:</b> "Due on receipt" is the norm in residential cleaning. You were in
    their home today; people pay the same week when the invoice is itemized and professional.</p>
    <p><b>Recurring residential:</b> same — invoice after each visit, or monthly for the month ahead if the
    client prefers predictability.</p>
    <p><b>Commercial clients:</b> they run on Net 15/Net 30 and will pay late regardless — invoice on the 1st
    for the month's visits and put the due date in a specific format ("Due by March 15") rather than "Net 30".
    Specific dates get paid faster than relative ones.</p>

    <h2>Make one in 30 seconds</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/cleaning/cleaning-invoice-generator/">Cleaning Invoice Generator</a> builds this exact
    layout in your browser — enter the client, add your service lines, and print or save as PDF. Nothing is
    uploaded or stored; no account, no watermark. Price the job first with the
    <a href="/guides/house-cleaning-prices/">cleaning pricing guide</a> or the
    <a href="/cleaning/cleaning-estimate-calculator/">estimate calculator</a>, and standardize the visit with
    the <a href="/cleaning/cleaning-checklist-builder/">checklist builder</a>.</p>

    <h2>Questions cleaners ask</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details><summary>Should a cleaning invoice include tax?</summary><p>Depends on your state: some states tax residential cleaning services, others don't, and a few tax only commercial. Check your state's rule once, then either add a tax line or note "tax not applicable". Getting this wrong is a fix-later problem, not a stop-everything problem.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Do I need invoice software?</summary><p>No. A clean one-page template you fill and print covers most residential and small-commercial needs. Software starts paying off when you need automatic recurring invoices and card payment links — usually past 15–20 recurring clients.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What if the client refuses to pay?</summary><p>Send one polite reminder at 7 days with the invoice attached, one firmer note at 14, then a final notice at 30 stating next steps. For residential amounts, small claims court is the realistic backstop. The best protection is upstream: itemized quotes and payment terms agreed in writing before the first visit.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Invoice before or after the clean?</summary><p>After, for one-time and residential — invoice on completion, same day. For recurring commercial, invoice on a fixed schedule (the 1st) for that month's visits. First-time commercial clients sometimes expect a deposit — 50% before the first clean is reasonable and common.</p></details>
    </div>
